      "math_academy_id": "e-5651",
      "problem": "Suppose that $A$ and $B$ are two events such that $P(A|B)=0.3,$ $P(A)=0.5,$ and $P(B)=0.6.$ Find $P(B|A).$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the multiplication law for probability, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cap B) & =P(B)\\cdot P(A|B) \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 0.3 \\\\  & =0.18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $P(A\\cap B) = 0.18$ and $P(A)=0.5$ into the definition of $P(B|A),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(B|A) & =\\frac{P(B\\cap A)}{P(A)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(A\\cap B)}{P(A)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.18}{0.5} \\\\  & =0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the fact that $P(A\\cap B) = P(B\\cap A).$",
